<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/gestational-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Screen for GDM in all women not known to have diabetes", "Gestational diabetes is a type of diabetes that develops during pregnancy.",
    "Diabetes that occurs only during pregnancy, called gestational diabetes, does not usually cause eye problems.",
    "Gestational diabetes is a type of diabetes that occurs only during pregnancy.", "If you have gestational diabetes, you can manage your blood glucose level by following a healthy eating plan and doing a moderate-intensity physical activity, such as brisk walking for 150 minutes, each week.",
    "Some people develop diabetes during pregnancy that goes away after they have the baby. This type of diabetes is called gestational diabetes.",
    "If you had gestational diabetes, your health care team will check your blood glucose levels in the first 3 months after having your baby to see if your gestational diabetes has completely gone away.",
    "People with a history of gestational diabetes are at higher risk of developing type 2 diabetes later in life.",
    "All pregnant women who do not have a previous diagnosis of diabetes should be tested for gestational diabetes between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy.",
    "If you are pregnant, your doctor might test you for gestational diabetes with the glucose challenge test." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/a1c> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Check A1C, set/review individualized goal",
    "In some cases, health care professionals use the A1C test to help confirm the results of another blood glucose test.",
    "Tests used to monitor your diabetes—A1C, blood pressure, and cholesterol—help your doctor decide whether it is important to do additional tests to check your heart health or to refer you to a specialist such as a cardiologist.",
    "Your doctor may recommend having eye exams more often than once a year, along with management of your diabetes. This means managing your diabetes ABCs, which include your A1c, blood pressure, and cholesterol; and quitting smoking.",
    "To prevent diabetic eye disease, or to keep it from getting worse, manage your diabetes ABCs: your A1c, blood pressure, and cholesterol; and quit smoking if you smoke.",
    "The A1C is a blood test that shows your average blood glucose level over the past 3 months. This is different from the blood glucose checks that you may do yourself. The higher your A1C number, the higher your blood glucose levels have been during the past 3 months.",
    "If your A1C test results and blood glucose levels do not match, your doctor should consider that the A1C test may not be a reliable test for you." .

