<https://example.org/dmo/id/adverseEffect/diabetic-ketoacidosis> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Individuals with type 1 diabetes must not have their insulin discontinued, as they will develop diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). This may occur in up to 25% of cases following metabolic-bariatric surgery.",
    "Do not stop insulin; risk of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/adverseEffect/hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Hypoglycaemia is defined as blood glucose <4 mmol/L. Prevention and management of hypoglycaemia must be considered when planning and during the perioperative and postoperative phases of surgery in all those at risk (on insulin and insulin secretagogues)",
    "Prevent hypoglycaemia (blood glucose <4 mmol/L) during all phases of care", "prevention of hypoglycaemia (blood glucose <4 mmol/L)",
    "It is also important to check for the occurrence of and frequency of hypoglycaemia when conducting reviews in the postoperative phase for all individuals with diabetes on sulfonylurea, meglitinides or insulin therapies.",
    "optimise glycaemic control and prevention of hypoglycaemia.", "If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately. Continue to follow the guidance below. Restart the insulin infusion once the hypo has been fully treated.",
    "Hypoglycaemia is the commonest side-effect of insulin or sulfonylurea therapy used to treat diabetes.",
    "People with type 1 diabetes mellitus (T1DM) experience around two episodes of mild hypoglycaemia per week.",
    "In unselected populations, the annual prevalence of severe hypoglycaemia has been reported consistently to be 30-40% in several large studies (11).",
    "Hypoglycaemia was given as the cause of admission for 5% of admissions for people with T1DM and 1.5% of admissions for people with T2DM (17).",
    "Over one third of 2000 ambulance call outs for severe hypoglycaemia represented repeat call outs, suggesting a significant minority are having recurrent problems (14).",
    "The risk of hypoglycaemia with sulfonylurea therapy is often underestimated and due to the duration of action of these tablets, is frequently prolonged.",
    "The UK Hypoglycaemia Group Study showed equivalent levels of severe hypoglycaemia in people with T2DM treated with sulfonylurea therapy compared with insulin therapy of less than two years duration (15).",
    "NaDIA (National Diabetes Inpatient Audit 2019) data shows a year on year reduction in the frequency of hypoglycaemic episodes experienced over the 7 days prior to the day of audit. However, 18.4% of people still reported one or more hypoglycaemic episodes (blood glucose <4.0mmol/L) with 7.0% reporting one or more hypoglycaemic episodes <3.0mmol/L.",
    "18.4% of people still reported one or more hypoglycaemic episodes (blood glucose <4.0mmol/L) with 7.0% reporting one or more hypoglycaemic episodes <3.0mmol/L.",
    "This may require oral or intravenous carbohydrate depending on the clinical situation; if the person is fasting for 30%",
    "hypoglycaemia and hypokalaemia were not infrequent if the infusion rate was kept at 0.1 unit/kg/hr ever though 10% glucose had been commenced.",
    "When the glucose concentrations drops to ≤14.0 mmol/L consider reducing the rate of intravenous insulin infusion to 0.05 units/kg/hour to reduce the risk of developing hypoglycaemia and / or hypokalaemia",
    "A national survey of DKA management following earlier version of this guideline found that the rates of hypoglycaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) and hypokalaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) were 27.6% and 67% respectively." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Prevent hypoglycaemia (blood glucose <4 mmol/L) during all phases of care", "It is also important to check for the occurrence of and frequency of hypoglycaemia when conducting reviews in the postoperative phase for all individuals with diabetes on sulfonylurea, meglitinides or insulin therapies.",
    "Hypoglycaemia is defined as blood glucose <4 mmol/L.", "If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately. Continue to follow the guidance below. Restart the insulin infusion once the hypo has been fully treated.",
    "The hospital management of hypoglycaemia in adults with diabetes mellitus", "Hypoglycaemia continues to be one of the most feared short-term complications of diabetes mellitus amongst people with diabetes, healthcare professionals and lay carers alike.",
    "Hypoglycaemia is a serious condition and should be treated as an emergency regardless of level of conciousness. Hypoglycaemia is defined as blood sugar glucose of <4.0mmol/L (if not <4.0mmol/L but symptomatic give a small carbohydrate snack for symptom relief)",
    """Treatment of hypoglycaemia algorithms

11
1. Quickly check the following.  Don’t spend too much time on this, particularly if the person""",
    "If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately. Continue to follow the guidance below.",
    "C. Adults who are unconscious and/or having seizures and/or are very aggressive",
    "If blood glucose remains less than 4.0mmol/L after 30-45 minutes (or 3 treatment cycles) give 100ml of 20% glucose at 400ml/hour or 200ml of 10% glucose at 800ml/hour over 15 minutes.",
    "Hypoglycaemia results from an imbalance between glucose supply, glucose utilisation and current insulin levels. Hypoglycaemia is the commonest side-effect of insulin or sulfonylurea therapy used to treat diabetes.",
    "Hypoglycaemia is a level of blood glucose below normal. Normal blood glucose levels in a person without diabetes are 3.5-7.0mmol/L. To avoid potential hypoglycaemia, Diabetes UK recommends a practical policy of “make four the floor”, i.e. 4.0mmol/L is the lowest acceptable blood glucose level in people with diabetes. In clinical practice, it can be defined as ”non-severe” if the episode is self-treated and “severe” if unable to self-treat and assistance by a third party is required (11).",
    "Hypoglycaemia with a glucose <3.0mmol/L recurred in in 39% and 45% of people with T2DM and T1DM respectively, in a study of over 17,000 hospital admissions (20).",
    "Hypoglycaemia can cause coma, hemiparesis and seizures. If the hypoglycaemia is prolonged the neurological deficits may become permanent.",
    "People with diabetes experiencing hypoglycaemia require a rapid-acting carbohydrate to restore euglycaemia (usually 4.0-7.0mmol/L).",
    """Management of hypoglycaemia

29
Computerised approaches to reducing hypoglycaemia""",
    "Hypoglycaemia requiring third party assistance and driving", "Glucose concentrations of less than 3.0 mmol/L (54 mg/dL) should be reported in clinical trials",
    "In patients with hypoglycemia is D50 too much of a good thing? J Emerg Med Serv. 2007;103–10.",
    "Hypoglycaemia Audit Form To be completed by a Healthcare Professional after each hypoglycaemic episode",
    "Indication: Management of adult hypoglycaemia, where dose should be prescribed by volume and concentration to minimise confusion.",
    "In all other situations, an infusion pump is required. Appendix 5", "A national survey of DKA management following earlier version of this guideline found that the rates of hypoglycaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) and hypokalaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) were 27.6% and 67% respectively.",
    "The blood glucose may fall very rapidly as ketoacidosis is corrected and a common mistake is to allow the blood glucose to drop to hypoglycaemic levels.",
    "The effect of hypoglycaemia during hospital admission on health-related outcomes for people with diabetes: a systematic review and meta-analysis." .

