<https://example.org/dmo/id/monitoringSchedule/eye-exam-for-type-1-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Type 1: Yearly eye exams should start within 5 years of diagnosis." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/monitoringSchedule/eye-exam-for-type-2-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Type 2: Yearly eye exams should start right after diagnosis." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/anti-vegf-medicine-for-diabetic-macular-edema>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Your doctor may treat your eyes with anti-VEGF medicine, such as aflibercept, bevacizumab, or ranibizumab. These medicines block the growth of abnormal blood vessels in the eye. Anti-VEGF medicines can also stop fluid leaks, which can help treat diabetic macular edema." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/cataract-lens-surgery> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "In a surgery center or hospital visit, your doctor can remove the cloudy lens in your eye, where the cataract has grown, and replace it with an artificial lens. People who have cataract surgery generally have better vision afterward." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/dilated-eye-exam-once-a-year> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "have a dilated eye exam once a year" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/eye-exam-before-pregnancy-for-women-with-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Women with type 1 and type 2 diabetes need an eye exam before pregnancy or within the first 3 months." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/focal-grid-laser-treatment> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Focal/grid laser treatment works on a small area of the retina to treat diabetic macular edema." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/get-help-to-quit-smoking> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "If you smoke, get help to quit smoking", "To help prevent diabetes-related nerve damage, get help to quit smoking." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/laser-treatment-for-leaky-blood-vessels-and-edema>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Laser treatment, also called photocoagulation, creates tiny burns inside the eye with a beam of light. This method treats leaky blood vessels and extra fluid, called edema." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/manage-blood-glucose-blood-pressure-and-cholesterol>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "manage your blood glucose, blood pressure, and cholesterol, sometimes called the diabetes ABCs" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/manage-diabetes-abcs> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "To prevent diabetic eye disease, or to keep it from getting worse, manage your diabetes ABCs: your A1c, blood pressure, and cholesterol; and quit smoking if you smoke." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/repeat-eye-exam-during-pregnancy-for-women-with-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Your doctor may want you to repeat the exam later in your pregnancy and until your baby is 1 year old." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/scatter-laser-treatment> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Scatter laser treatment, also called panretinal photocoagulation (PRP), covers a larger area of the retina. This method treats the growth of abnormal blood vessels, called proliferative diabetic retinopathy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/vitrectomy> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Vitrectomy is a surgery to remove the clear gel that fills the center of the eye, called the vitreous gel. The procedure treats problems with severe bleeding or scar tissue caused by proliferative diabetic retinopathy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/yearly-eye-exams-for-type-1-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Type 1: Yearly eye exams should start within 5 years of diagnosis." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/yearly-eye-exams-for-type-2-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Type 2: Yearly eye exams should start right after diagnosis." .

