<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/charcot-s-foot> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Although rare, nerve damage from diabetes can lead to changes in the shape of your feet, such as Charcot’s foot. Charcot’s foot may start with redness, warmth, and swelling. Later, bones in your feet and toes can shift or break, which can cause your feet to have an odd shape, such as a “rocker bottom.”" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/foot-ulcer> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "a cut, blister, or bruise on your foot that does not start to heal after a few days" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/gangrene> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "The infection might lead to gangrene.", "a foot infection that becomes black and smelly—signs you might have gangrene" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/peripheral-artery-disease> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "peripheral artery disease" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/peripheral-neuropathy> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "If you have nerve damage from diabetes, you may burn your feet and not know you did",
    "Peripheral neuropathy is nerve damage that typically affects the feet and legs and sometimes affects the hands and arms.",
    "Foot care is very important for all people with diabetes, and it’s even more important if you have peripheral neuropathy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/contraindication/cutting-corns-and-calluses> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Do NOT cut corns and calluses" .

