    "Occasionally, DKA may affect people with type 2 diabetes who have lost their ability to produce insulin.",
    "If you have gestational diabetes, you are more likely to develop type 2 diabetes later in life." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/adverseEffect/upset-stomach> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Ask your doctor whether your diabetes medicine can cause hypoglycemia or other side effects, such as upset stomach and weight gain." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/adverseEffect/weight-gain> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Ask your doctor whether your diabetes medicine can cause hypoglycemia or other side effects, such as upset stomach and weight gain." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/device/artificial-pancreas> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "An artificial pancreas—also called an automated insulin delivery system—may be another option for some people.",
    "An artificial pancreas is a system of three devices that work together to mimic how a healthy pancreas controls blood glucose in the body.",
    "Some people use a CGM as part of an artificial pancreas. An artificial pancreas is a system that mimics how a healthy pancreas controls blood glucose in the body. A CGM, an insulin pump, and a software program that shares information between the CGM and insulin pump make up the artificial pancreas." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/device/insulin-infusion-pump> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "The insulin infusion pump will adjust how much insulin is given from minute to minute to help keep your blood glucose level in your target range.",
    "** If ketones and glucose are not falling as expected always check the insulin infusion pump is working and connected and that the correct insulin residual volume is present (to check for pump malfunction)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/glucagon-like-peptide-1-receptor-agonists> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "These medicines, known as glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) receptor agonists,3 may make you feel less hungry and help you lose some weight. GLP-1 medicines are not substitutes for insulin.",
    "Other medicines, called glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor (GLP-1) agonists, increase insulin production and decrease your appetite." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/intermediate-acting-insulin> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "intermediate-acting 2 to 4 hours 4 to 12 hours 12 to 18 hours" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/long-acting-insulin> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "long-acting 2 hours does not peak 24 hours", "If the hypoglycaemia was caused by sulfonylurea or long-acting insulin therapy then be aware the risk of hypoglycaemia may persist for up to 24-36 hours following the last dose, especially if there is concurrent renal impairment.",
    "After acute treatment, consideration should be given to whether the hypoglycaemia is likely to be prolonged, i.e. as a result of long-acting insulin or sulfonylurea therapy; these people may require a continuous infusion of glucose to maintain blood glucose levels.",
    "If the person was previously on a long acting insulin such as glargine, degludec, detemir or human isophane, this should have been continued" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/premixed-insulin> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Premixed insulin starts to work in 15 to 60 minutes and can last from 10 to 16 hours." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/rapid-acting-inhaled-insulin> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "rapid-acting, inhaled 10 to 15 minutes 30 minutes 3 hours" .

