<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/glucose-challenge-test-for-gestational-diabetes-screening>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If you are pregnant, your doctor might test you for gestational diabetes with the glucose challenge test." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/oral-glucose-tolerance-test-for-type-2-and-gestational-diabetes-detection>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "The oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) helps doctors detect type 2 diabetes, prediabetes, and gestational diabetes." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/random-plasma-glucose-test-for-diabetes-diagnosis>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Sometimes doctors use the random plasma glucose test to diagnose diabetes when you have symptoms of diabetes and they do not want to wait until you have fasted for 8 hours." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/retesting-adults-and-children-with-normal-diabetes-test-results>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Adults and children with normal diabetes test results should be retested every 3 years." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/routine-testing-for-type-2-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Experts recommend routine testing for type 2 diabetes if you have certain risk factors." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/testing-after-delivery-for-women-with-gestational-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If you have gestational diabetes, you should get tested after your baby is born to see if you have type 2 diabetes, usually within 12 weeks after delivery." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/testing-all-pregnant-women-for-gestational-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "All pregnant women who do not have a previous diagnosis of diabetes should be tested for gestational diabetes between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/testing-children-and-teens-for-type-2-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Experts recommend testing children and teens between the ages of 10 and 18 who are overweight or have obesity and have at least one more risk factor, such as a low birth weight, a parent who had diabetes while pregnant, or another factor." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/testing-pregnant-women-for-type-2-diabetes-at-first-prenatal-appointment>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If you are already pregnant, you should be tested for type 2 diabetes at the first prenatal appointment, or in the first 15 weeks of pregnancy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/testing-women-planning-to-get-pregnant-for-type-2-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "All women who are planning to get pregnant should be tested for type 2 diabetes, especially if they have risk factors." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-ogtt-for-gestational-diabetes-diagnosis>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If you are pregnant, your blood will be drawn every hour for 2 to 3 hours. If your blood glucose levels are high two or more times during the OGTT, you may have gestational diabetes." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/a-parent-who-had-diabetes-while-pregnant> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "a parent who had diabetes while pregnant" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/a-woman-who-had-gestational-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "a woman who had gestational diabetes" .

