<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/slgt-2-inhibitors> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "metformin, pioglitazone, DPP-4 inhibitors, acarbose, SLGT-2 inhibitors and GLP-1 analogues prescribed without insulin or an insulin secretagogue (sulfonylurea and repaglinide) are unlikely to result in hypoglycaemia." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/sulfonylurea-therapy> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "The risk of hypoglycaemia with sulfonylurea therapy is often underestimated and due to the duration of action of these tablets, is frequently prolonged.",
    "Nocturnal hypoglycaemia (blood glucose <3.9mmol/L) was more common in people treated with sulfonylurea therapy than insulin therapy (75.3% versus 59.3% respectively).",
    "An 84-year-old woman with T2DM on high dose sulfonylurea therapy admitted with a urinary tract infection and acute kidney injury would be at substantially higher risk of hypoglycaemia.",
    "After acute treatment, consideration should be given to whether the hypoglycaemia is likely to be prolonged, i.e. as a result of long-acting insulin or sulfonylurea therapy; these people may require a continuous infusion of glucose to maintain blood glucose levels.",
    "Glucagon is only licensed for insulin induced hypoglycaemia and may be less effective in people prescribed sulfonylurea therapy (may take up to 15 minutes to take effect). Glucagon mobilises glycogen from the liver and will be less effective in those who are chronically malnourished (including those who have had a prolonged period of starvation), abuse alcohol or have chronic liver disease.",
    "The gap was largest in those with T2DM taking sulfonylurea therapy and smallest in those with T1DM" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose-level> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Hypoglycaemia is a level of blood glucose below normal.  Normal blood glucose levels in a person without diabetes are 3.5-7.0mmol/L." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "A Scottish study examined the gap between recognition of hypoglycaemia (by capillary blood glucose monitoring) and repeat blood glucose monitoring",
    "Repeat capillary blood glucose measurement 10-15 minutes later. If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L, repeat step 3 (no more than 3 treatments in total).",
    "Repeat capillary blood glucose levels after 10-15 minutes.  If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L repeat steps 1 and 3 (no more than 3 treatments in total and only give IM glucagon once).",
    "Repeat capillary blood glucose measurement 10 minutes later. If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L, repeat step 5 (or 6 if IV access remains unavailable).",
    "Repeat capillary blood glucose measurement 10 to 15 minutes later. If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L, repeat step 3 (no more than 3 treatments in total).",
    "MONITORING HOURLY CBG, CAPILLARY KETONES, Urinary Output", "• capillary blood glucose above 11 mmol/L",
    "Reduce capillary blood glucose by 3.0 mmol/L/hour", "CBG not falling by at least 3 mmol/L/hr",
    "1. Capillary blood glucose (CBG) 11.0mmol/L or known diabetes" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-glucose-measurement> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "For this reason, a capillary blood glucose measurement forms part of the systematic “ABCDE” initial approach to the management of the acutely ill adult (9)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/hypoglycaemia-2-9mmol-l> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
