<https://example.org/dmo/id/medication/sulfonylurea> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Hypoglycaemia is the commonest side-effect of insulin or sulfonylurea therapy used to treat diabetes. Because of their modes of action (i.e. they prevent glucose from rising rather than lowering glucose concentrations); metformin, pioglitazone, DPP-4 inhibitors, acarbose, SLGT-2 inhibitors and GLP-1 analogues prescribed without insulin or an insulin secretagogue (sulfonylurea and repaglinide) are unlikely to result in hypoglycaemia." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/medication/sulfonylurea-therapy> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Nocturnal hypoglycaemia (blood glucose <3.9mmol/L) was more common in people treated with sulfonylurea therapy than insulin therapy (75.3% versus 59.3% respectively).",
    "If the hypoglycaemia was caused by sulfonylurea or long-acting insulin therapy then be aware the risk of hypoglycaemia may persist for up to 24-36 hours following the last dose, especially if there is concurrent renal impairment.",
    "An 84-year-old woman with T2DM on high dose sulfonylurea therapy admitted" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/monitoringSchedule/capillary-blood-glucose-monitoring>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Ensure regular capillary blood glucose monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/monitoringSchedule/regular-capillary-blood-glucose-monitoring>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Ensure regular capillary blood glucose monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/10-or-20-glucose-solutions-preferred-for-intravenous-administration>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "For these reasons, 10% or 20% glucose solutions are preferred for intravenous administration." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/adjust-insulin-pump-settings-after-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "8. People who self-manage their insulin pumps (CSII) may not need a long-acting carbohydrate, but should take initial treatment as outlined and adjust their pump settings appropriately. Many people will have a locally devised hypoglycaemia protocol that should be checked to ensure it remains appropriate for use in the inpatient setting.",
    "10. People who self-manage their insulin pumps (CSII) may not need a long-acting carbohydrate, but should take initial treatment as outlined and adjust their pump settings appropriately. Many people will have a locally devised hypoglycaemia protocol that should be checked to ensure it remains appropriate for use in the inpatient setting." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/adjust-variable-rate-intravenous-insulin-infusion-as-per-prescribed-regimen-and-seek-medical-advice>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If the person has a variable rate intravenous insulin infusion, adjust as per prescribed regimen, and seek medical advice." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/administer-10-iv-glucose-at-100ml-hr> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    """6. Once blood glucose is above 4.0mmol/L and the person has recovered:
• Restart feed
• If bolus feeding, give additional bolus feed (read nutritional information and 
calculate amount required to give 20g of carbohydrate)
• 10% IV glucose at 100ml/hr.  Volume should be determined by clinical 
circumstances (refer to Appendix 5 for administration details)""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/administer-100ml-of-20-glucose-at-400ml-hour-or-200ml-of-10-glucose-at-800ml-hour-over-15-minutes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "5. If IV access is available give 100ml of 20% glucose at 400ml/hour or 200ml of 10% glucose at 800ml/hour over 15 minutes. If an infusion pump is available then use this, but if not available the infusion should not be delayed. Care should be taken with infusion pump settings if larger volume bags are used to ensure the whole bag is not inadvertently administered. The smallest possible volume should be administered in renal and/or cardiac failure." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/administer-1mg-glucagon-im-if-no-iv-access-available>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "6. If no IV access is available then give 1mg Glucagon IM. Glucagon is only licensed for insulin induced hypoglycaemia and may be less effective in people prescribed sulfonylurea therapy (may take up to 15 minutes to take effect). Glucagon mobilises glycogen from the liver and will be less effective in those who are chronically malnourished (including those who have had a prolonged period of starvation), abuse alcohol or have chronic liver disease. In this situation IV glucose is the preferred option. If no IV access is available initially, continue trying to achieve IV access as IM glucagon is less likely to be successful if required for a second time." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/administer-glucagon-or-iv-glucose-if-hypoglycaemia-persists>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "5. If blood glucose remains less than 4.0mmol/L after 30-45 minutes or 3 treatment cycles, call for medical assistance. If agreed locally, glucagon (and IV glucose) may be given without prescription in an emergency for the purpose of saving a life or via a Patient Group Directive. Consider: a. 1mg of glucagon IM (only licensed for insulin induced hypoglycaemia, may be less effective when administered repeatedly, in people prescribed sulfonylurea therapy or people with a history of alcohol abuse or chronic liver disease) b. 100ml of 20% glucose (at 400ml/hour over 15 minutes) or 200ml of 10% glucose (at 800ml/hour over 15 minutes).  Care should be taken with infusion pump settings if larger volume bags are used to ensure the whole bag is not inadvertently administered.  Consider smallest possible volume in renal and/or cardiac failure" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/administer-iv-glucose-or-im-glucagon-without-prescription-in-emergency>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "4. If agreed locally, IV glucose or IM glucagon may be given without prescription in an emergency for the purpose of saving a life or via a Patient Group Directive." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/administer-rapid-acting-carbohydrate-for-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "3. Give 15-20g rapid-acting carbohydrate of the person with diabetes in hospital’s choice where possible.  Some examples are: a. 5-7 Dextrosol® tablets (or 4-5 Lift GlucoTabs™) b. 1 bottle (60ml) Lift juice shots c. 150-200ml pure fruit juice (e.g. orange juice), do not use if following a low potassium diet (e.g. to treat chronic kidney disease) in view of its potassium content d. 3-4 heaped teaspoons of sugar dissolved in water (sugar dissolved in water is not an effective treatment for people taking acarbose as it prevents the breakdown of sucrose to glucose)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/administration-of-iv-glucose-in-emergency>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Give 100ml of 20% glucose at 400ml/hour or 200ml of 10% glucose at 800ml/hour over 15 minutes. Care should be taken with infusion pump settings if larger volume bags are used to ensure the whole bag is not inadvertently administered. Consider smallest possible volume in renal impairment and/or cardiac failure." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/alert-value-for-blood-glucose-in-hospital>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "For the majority of people with diabetes in hospital, a blood glucose between 4.0-6.0mmol/L should act as an alert value, especially if a repeated finding." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/any-blood-glucose-less-than-4-0mmol-l-should-be-treated-during-hospital-admission>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "During a hospital admission, any blood glucose less than 4.0mmol/L should be treated." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/availability-of-iv-glucose-preparation>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Chosen preparation of IV glucose should also be included or kept nearby with appropriate giving set." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/availability-of-quick-and-long-acting-carbohydrate-in-clinical-area>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Easily accessible quick- and long-acting carbohydrate must be available in your clinical area (ideally located in a hypo box) and all staff should be aware of its location." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/availability-of-sharps-disposal-equipment>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Appropriate portable sharps disposal equipment should also be kept nearby." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/avoid-chocolate-for-treatment-of-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Chocolate is no longer recommended for the treatment of hypoglycaemia. Chocolate contains rapid-acting carbohydrate and fat; the addition of fat has been shown to slow the absorption of quick-acting carbohydrate (50,51)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/avoid-extravasation-during-glucose-infusion>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Avoid extravasation – may cause tissue damage" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/awareness-of-prolonged-hypoglycaemia-risk-after-sulfonylurea-or-long-acting-insulin>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If the hypoglycaemia was caused by sulfonylurea or long-acting insulin therapy then be aware the risk of hypoglycaemia may persist for up to 24-36 hours following the last dose, especially if there is concurrent renal impairment." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/be-aware-of-persistent-hypoglycaemia-risk-for-up-to-24-36-hours-after-sulfonylurea-or-long-acting-insulin-therapy>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If the hypoglycaemia was caused by sulfonylurea or long-acting insulin therapy then be aware the risk of hypoglycaemia may persist for up to 24-36 hours following the last dose, especially if there is concurrent renal impairment." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/be-aware-of-prolonged-hypoglycaemia-risk-after-sulfonylurea-or-long-acting-insulin-therapy>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "13. If the hypoglycaemia was caused by sulfonylurea or long-acting insulin therapy then be aware the risk of hypoglycaemia may persist for up to 24-36 hours following the last dose, especially if there is concurrent renal impairment." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/be-aware-of-prolonged-hypoglycaemia-risk-with-sulfonylurea-or-long-acting-insulin>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """8. If the hypoglycaemia was caused by sulfonylurea or long-acting insulin therapy then be 
aware the risk of hypoglycaemia may persist for up to 24-36 hours following the last 
dose, especially if there is concurrent renal impairment.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/blood-glucose-levels-between-4-0-6-0mmol-l-may-indicate-looming-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Blood glucose levels between 4.0-6.0mmol/L may indicate looming hypoglycaemia and require special consideration; see section on glycaemic targets for people with diabetes in hospital for further details" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/blood-glucose-measurement-for-hypoglycaemia-confirmation>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Where it is safe to do so, a blood glucose measurement should be taken to confirm hypoglycaemia (especially if there is any suspicion the person may also be under the influence of alcohol)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/blood-glucose-monitoring-for-asymptomatic-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Regular blood glucose monitoring enables detection of asymptomatic biochemical hypoglycaemia." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/call-for-medical-assistance-if-hypoglycaemia-persists>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If blood glucose remains less than 4.0mmol/L after 30-45 minutes or 3 treatment cycles, call for medical assistance. If agreed locally, IV glucose may be given without prescription in an emergency for the purpose of saving a life or via a Patient Group Directive." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/capillary-blood-glucose-monitoring-after-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Ensure regular capillary blood glucose monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/carbohydrate-counting-using-10g-bricks>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Allowing people to easily identify the amount of carbohydrate consumed (by providing easily identified 10g “bricks” of carbohydrate) can allow them to carbohydrate count." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/change-of-insulin-injection-site> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "• Change of insulin injection site" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/cleaning-of-hypo-box> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "A hard plastic hypo box can be cleaned with a damp cloth or clinell wipe, and should be cleaned in accordance with your Trust’s equipment cleaning procedures." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/communicate-significant-diabetes-treatment-changes-or-monitoring-recommendations>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """l	 If the diabetes treatment has changed significantly on discharge, or enhanced 
monitoring has been recommended, please communicate this to their primary 
care team in a timely manner.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/communication-with-primary-care-after-significant-treatment-changes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Please consider how best to communicate with colleagues in primary care if there have been significant treatment changes. This would be particularly important if treatment changes were temporary and required review (e.g. withholding a sulfonylurea and metformin after hypoglycaemia during a period of acute kidney injury)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/complete-incident-form-if-appropriate>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """When hypoglycaemia has been successfully treated  
(after following the initial steps outlined in algorithms A-E) 

l	 Consider completing an incident form (e.g. DATIX) if appropriate.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/concurrent-use-of-medicines-with-hypoglycaemic-agents>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "l Concurrent use of medicines with hypoglycaemic agents e.g. warfarin, quinine, salicylates, fibrates, sulphonamides (including cotrimoxazole), linezolid, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), probenecid, somatostatin analogues, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors. Do not stop or withhold medication, discuss with the medical team or pharmacist" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/consider-dose-adjustment-in-high-risk-individuals-with-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "An 84-year-old woman with T2DM on high dose sulfonylurea therapy admitted with a urinary tract infection and acute kidney injury would be at substantially higher risk of hypoglycaemia. Dose adjustment may well then be appropriate, possibly proactively but certainly if readings in the range of 4.0-6.0mmol/L were being recorded." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/consider-intravenous-infusion-of-10-glucose-at-100ml-hr-until-no-longer-nil-by-mouth-or-reviewed-by-medical-team>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Once blood glucose is greater than 4.0mmol/L and the person has recovered consider intravenous infusion of 10% glucose at a rate of 100ml/hr until they are no longer ‘Nil by Mouth’ or have been reviewed by the medical team." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/consider-medical-or-disn-review-if-cause-not-identifiable>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """When hypoglycaemia has been successfully treated  
(after following the initial steps outlined in algorithms A-E) 

l	 Consider completing an incident form (e.g. DATIX) if appropriate. 
l	 If “hypo boxes” have been used then restock as appropriate. 
l	 Identify the risk factors for hypoglycaemia (see Table 2). 
l	 Take measures to avoid hypoglycaemia in the future.  The Inpatient Diabetes 
Team can be contacted to discuss this. 
l	 Unless the cause is easily identifiable and both the nursing staff and the person 
with diabetes is  confident steps can be taken to avoid future events, a medical 
 or Diabetes Inpatient Specialist Nurse (DISN) review should be considered.  If the 
hypoglycaemia event was severe or recurrent, or if the person voices concerns 
then a review is indicated""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/consider-reducing-insulin-or-sulfonylurea-dose-active-at-time-of-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """l	 Medical team (or DISN if referred) to consider reducing the dose of insulin or 
sulfonylurea that would have been active at the time of the hypoglycaemic event.  
This is to prevent further hypoglycaemia episodes occurring.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/consideration-of-prolonged-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "After acute treatment, consideration should be given to whether the hypoglycaemia is likely to be prolonged, i.e. as a result of long-acting insulin or sulfonylurea therapy; these people may require a continuous infusion of glucose to maintain blood glucose levels." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/continue-blood-glucose-monitoring-for-24-to-48-hours-after-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "14. Document event in the individual’s clinical record. Ensure regular capillary blood glucose monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours. Ask the person to continue this at home if they are to be discharged." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/continue-capillary-blood-glucose-monitoring-for-at-least-24-to-48-hours>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Ensure regular capillary blood glucose monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/continue-iv-insulin-after-hypoglycaemia-recovery-with-dose-review>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "12. If the person was on IV insulin, continue to check blood glucose every 15 minutes until above 4.0mmol/L, then re-start IV insulin after review of dose regimen to try and prevent hypoglycaemia recurrence. Consider concurrent IV 10% glucose infusion at 100ml/hr and/or stepping down the insulin increments on the variable scale if appropriate (check local Trust guidance)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/contraindication-of-im-and-subcutaneous-injection-for-glucose>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "IM injection: Contraindicated Subcutaneous injection: Contraindicated" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/daily-check-of-hypo-box> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Appendix 1 “Hypo box” contents should be checked on a daily basis to ensure it is complete and in date. It is the responsibility of the member of staff who uses any contents to replenish them after use." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/discuss-subsequent-diabetes-treatment-if-unsure>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """l	 Please DO NOT omit the next insulin injection or start variable rate intravenous 
insulin infusion to ‘stabilise’ blood glucose.  If unsure of subsequent diabetes 
treatment, discuss with the inpatient diabetes team (e.g. it may be safe to omit a 
meal time bolus dose of rapid-acting insulin if the person has declined food and 
taken their usual basal insulin).""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/do-not-omit-insulin-injection-if-due> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "9. DO NOT omit insulin injection if due.  The insulin injection about to be given is unlikely to be the insulin dose that was active at the time of the hypoglycaemia episode and so should not be omitted.  However, consideration will have to be given as to which insulin dose was active at the time of the hypoglycaemic episode and so a review of their insulin regimen is likely to be required.",
    """7. DO NOT omit insulin injection if due. The insulin injection about to be given is 
unlikely to be the insulin dose that was active at the time of the hypoglycaemia episode 
and so should not be omitted.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/do-not-omit-insulin-injection-if-due-after-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "11. DO NOT omit insulin injection if due. The insulin injection about to be given is unlikely to be the insulin dose that was active at the time of the hypoglycaemia episode and so should not be omitted. However, consideration will have to be given as to which insulin dose was active at the time of the hypoglycaemic episode and so a review of their insulin regimen is likely to be required." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/do-not-omit-next-insulin-injection-or-start-variable-rate-iv-insulin-infusion>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """l	 Please DO NOT omit the next insulin injection or start variable rate intravenous 
insulin infusion to ‘stabilise’ blood glucose.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/do-not-treat-isolated-hyperglycaemia-spikes-with-stat-doses-of-rapid-acting-insulin>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """l	 Please DO NOT treat isolated spikes of hyperglycaemia with ‘stat’ doses of rapid-
acting insulin. Instead maintain regular capillary blood glucose monitoring and 
adjust normal insulin or sulfonylurea doses only if a particular pattern emerges.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/document-hypoglycaemia-event-and-continue-monitoring>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "11. Document event in the individual’s clinical record. Ensure regular capillary blood glucose monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours.  Ask the person to continue this at home if they are to be discharged.  Give hypoglycaemia education or refer to local Diabetes Inpatient Team.  An education leaflet is available from the Trend Diabetes website (https://trenddiabetes.online/) (log in required).",
    """9. Document event in the individual’s clinical record. Ensure regular capillary blood glucose 
monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours.  Ask the person to continue this 
at home if they are to be discharged.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/document-hypoglycaemia-event-in-clinical-record>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "14. Document event in the individual’s clinical record. Ensure regular capillary blood glucose monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours. Ask the person to continue this at home if they are to be discharged. Give hypoglycaemia education or refer to local Diabetes Inpatient Team. An education leaflet is available from the Trend Diabetes website (https://trenddiabetes.online/) (log in required)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/documentation-of-hypoglycaemia-event> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Document event in the individual’s clinical record." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/endocrine-disorders-associated-with-diminished-counterregulatory-responses>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "l Endocrine disorders associated with diminished counterregulatory responses (e.g. Addison’s disease, growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/exclude-hypoglycaemia-in-acutely-unwell-adults-with-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Hypoglycaemia must be excluded in any person with diabetes who is acutely unwell, drowsy, unconscious, unable to co-operate, presenting with aggressive behaviour or a seizure." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/failure-to-monitor-blood-glucose-adequately>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "• Failure to monitor blood glucose adequately" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/flush-feeding-tube-with-40-50ml-water-after-carbohydrate-treatment>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "All treatments should be followed by a 40-50ml water flush of the feeding tube to prevent tube blockage (2)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/fruit-juices-and-products-specifically-designed-for-the-treatment-of-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Fruit juices and products specifically designed for the treatment of hypoglycaemia only, will be exempt from this sugar tax and are therefore recommended in this guideline." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/give-15-20g-rapid-acting-carbohydrate-via-feeding-tube-where-possible>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Give 15-20g rapid-acting carbohydrate of their choice where possible." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/give-additional-bolus-feed-if-bolus-feeding>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """6. Once blood glucose is above 4.0mmol/L and the person has recovered:
• Restart feed
• If bolus feeding, give additional bolus feed (read nutritional information and 
calculate amount required to give 20g of carbohydrate)""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/give-hypoglycaemia-education-or-refer-to-local-diabetes-inpatient-team>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Give hypoglycaemia education or refer to local Diabetes Inpatient Team." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/hypo-box-contents> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Example contents of a hypo box l Copy of hypoglycaemia algorithm (laminated and attached to inside of lid) l 2x 200ml carton of pure fruit juice l 2x packets of dextrose tablets l 1x mini pack of biscuits (source of long-acting carbohydrate) l 3 x tubes (1 box) 40% glucose gel l 20% glucose IV solution (100ml vial) with infusion set l 1x green cannula 18G l 1x grey cannula 16G l 1x 10ml sterile syringe l 3 x 10ml sodium chloride 0.9% ampoules for flush l 1x green sterile needle 21G l Chlorhexidine spray/alcohol wipes l 1x IV dressing (cannula cover) l 10% glucose for IV infusion (500ml bag) with infusion set l Audit form l Instructions on where to send audit form and replenish supplies l 1x Glucagon pack – to be kept in the nearest drug fridge or labelled with reduced expiry date of 18 months if stored at room temperature" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/hypo-boxes-should-be-available-in-clinical-areas>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Easily accessible quick- and long-acting carbohydrate must be available in your clinical area (ideally located in a hypo box) and all staff should be aware of its location." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/hypoglycaemia-education-or-referral-to-diabetes-inpatient-team>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Give hypoglycaemia education or refer to local Diabetes Inpatient Team." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/hypoglycaemia-must-be-excluded-in-acutely-unwell-people-with-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Hypoglycaemia must be excluded in any person with diabetes who is acutely unwell, drowsy, unconscious, unable to co-operate, presenting with aggressive behaviour or a seizure." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/identify-risk-factors-for-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """When hypoglycaemia has been successfully treated  
(after following the initial steps outlined in algorithms A-E) 

l	 Consider completing an incident form (e.g. DATIX) if appropriate. 
l	 If “hypo boxes” have been used then restock as appropriate. 
l	 Identify the risk factors for hypoglycaemia (see Table 2).""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/immediate-management-of-unconscious-or-seizure-prone-adults-with-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "1. Check: a. Airway (and give oxygen) b. Breathing c. Circulation d. Disability (including Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) and blood glucose) e. Exposure (including temperature)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/immediate-treatment-of-hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "All people experiencing hypoglycaemia should be treated without delay." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/increased-long-acting-carbohydrate-after-glucagon-use>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "People given glucagon require a larger portion of long-acting carbohydrate (40g) to replenish glycogen stores (double the suggested amounts below) although nausea associated with glucagon injections may be an issue." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/individualised-approach-to-hypoglycaemia-management>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "An individualised approach is often helpful and advice should be sought from the inpatient diabetes team if unsure." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/individualised-glycaemic-targets-for-insulin-pump-users-and-wearable-glucose-sensor-users>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "For some people, especially those using insulin pumps and/or wearable glucose sensors, a range of 4-6mmol/L may be their normal when they are not eating. It is important to have a discussion with them about the need to avoid severe hypoglycaemia, they may need to aim for higher glucose levels than they are used to." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/insulin-injection-should-not-be-omitted>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "DO NOT omit insulin injection if due. The insulin injection about to be given is unlikely to be the insulin dose that was active at the time of the hypoglycaemia episode and so should not be omitted." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/intervention-for-looming-hypoglycaemia-in-blood-glucose-range-4-0-6-0mmol-l>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "The risk of looming hypoglycaemia should be considered in people who have a blood glucose level of 4.0-6.0mmol/L while in hospital. For the majority of people on any insulin preparation and/or any insulin secretagogues, consider intervening at a capillary blood glucose (CBG) of <6.0mmol/L to prevent hypoglycaemia." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/intravenous-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "However, it can only be used once with the slower recovery and higher treatment failure rate making intravenous glucose the preferred option (60)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/iv-glucose-or-glucagon-can-be-given-without-a-prescription-in-emergency>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "It is important to emphasise that in the emergency situation IV glucose or glucagon can be given without a prescription to treat hypoglycaemia but that your local organisation might not permit this (69)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/joint-decision-on-intervention-threshold-for-blood-glucose>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "The decision as to whether to intervene at a blood glucose of <6.0 or <5.0mmol/L should ideally be a joint decision between the person and inpatient diabetes team." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/joint-decision-on-intervention-threshold-for-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "The decision as to whether to intervene at a blood glucose of <6.0 or <5.0mmol/L should ideally be a joint decision between the individual and the inpatient diabetes team." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/local-guidance-for-self-management-of-hypoglycaemia-should-be-agreed-with-other-medical-conditions>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "You may want to agree local guidance for the self-management of hypoglycaemia in conjunction with certain other medical conditions (e.g. renal impairment, congestive cardiac failure)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/major-amputation-of-a-limb> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "• Major amputation of a limb" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/management-of-adult-hypoglycaemia-with-10-or-20-glucose-infusion>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Indication: Management of adult hypoglycaemia, where dose should be prescribed by volume and concentration to minimise confusion." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/mobilisation-after-illness> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "• Mobilisation after illness" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/monitor-for-fluid-and-electrolyte-disturbances>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Monitor patients with, or at risk of, fluid overload" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/monitor-for-hyperglycaemia-during-glucose-infusion>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Hyperglycaemia (monitor blood glucose)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/monitor-for-persistent-hypoglycaemia-risk-after-sulfonylurea-or-long-acting-insulin>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "10. If the hypoglycaemia was caused by sulfonylurea or long-acting insulin therapy then be aware the risk of hypoglycaemia may persist for up to 24-36 hours following the last dose, especially if there is concurrent renal impairment." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/monitor-infusion-site-for-phlebitis> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Monitor the infusion site: if any signs of phlebitis, stop infusion, remove cannula and re-site" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/nasal-glucagon> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "More recently nasal glucagon has been suggested as an alternative with ease and success of administration reported as significantly better; particularly among lay caregivers. Efficacy in terms of resolution of hypoglycaemia was comparable with IM glucagon." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/observe-for-mental-confusion-and-loss-of-consciousness>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Observe patients for any sign of mental confusion and loss of consciousness" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/people-capable-of-self-care-should-alert-nursing-staff-when-hypoglycaemia-occurs>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "People capable of self-care should alert nursing staff an episode of hypoglycaemia has occurred so their management plan can be altered if necessary." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/prescribe-iv-glucose-preparation-on-an-as-required-prn-basis>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "The authors recommend the IV glucose preparation chosen is prescribed on an ‘as required’ (PRN) basis for all people with diabetes in hospital." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/proactive-intervention-for-looming-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "While the treatment threshold for hypoglycaemia remains at less than 4.0mmol/L, for people with diabetes in hospital, it may be appropriate to act proactively to prevent hypoglycaemia at higher blood glucose levels. This would be especially relevant for those on glucose lowering medications such as insulin secretagogues (e.g. sulfonylureas) or insulin therapy. A blood glucose value between 4.0 to 6.0mmol/L could indicate looming hypoglycaemia; particularly if repeated episodes showed a particular pattern of glucose measurements below 6.0mmol/L were occurring. This may require oral or intravenous carbohydrate depending on the clinical situation; if the person is fasting for" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/promotion-of-hypoglycaemia-rescue-treatment-prescription>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Promote the prescription of hypoglycaemia “rescue” treatment for all people with diabetes in hospital on insulin or sulfonylurea therapy. This could involve a “PRN (pro re nata)” or “as needed” prescription of intravenous (IV) glucose and/or intramuscular (IM) glucagon." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/provide-hypoglycaemia-education-or-refer-to-diabetes-inpatient-team>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """9. Document event in the individual’s clinical record. Ensure regular capillary blood glucose 
monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours.  Ask the person to continue this 
at home if they are to be discharged.  Give hypoglycaemia education or refer to local 
Diabetes Inpatient Team.  An education leaflet is available from the Trend Diabetes 
website (https://trenddiabetes.online/) (log in required).""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/provide-hypoglycaemia-education-or-refer-to-local-diabetes-inpatient-team>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "14. Document event in the individual’s clinical record. Ensure regular capillary blood glucose monitoring is continued for at least 24 to 48 hours. Ask the person to continue this at home if they are to be discharged. Give hypoglycaemia education or refer to local Diabetes Inpatient Team. An education leaflet is available from the Trend Diabetes website (https://trenddiabetes.online/) (log in required)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/rapid-acting-carbohydrate-for-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "People with diabetes experiencing hypoglycaemia require a rapid-acting carbohydrate to restore euglycaemia (usually 4.0-7.0mmol/L). The rapid-acting carbohydrate should be followed up by giving long-acting carbohydrate either as a snack or as part of a planned meal." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/rate-of-administration-for-20-glucose-infusion>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Give 100ml of 20% glucose * over 10-15 minutes (set rate 300-400mL/hour), or 200ml 10% glucose over 10-15 minutes (set rate 600-800mL/hour). Use an infusion pump if available. For the initial emergency management of hypoglycaemia this may be administered via a giving set alone." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/re-start-feed-after-hypoglycaemia-treatment>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """6. Once blood glucose is above 4.0mmol/L and the person has recovered:
• Restart feed""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/re-start-feed-to-rapidly-deliver-15-20g-carbohydrate>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Re-start feed to rapidly deliver 15 – 20g carbohydrate" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/recovery-from-acute-illness-stress> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "• Recovery from acute illness/stress" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/refer-to-dietitian-nutrition-team-and-or-inpatient-diabetes-team-for-adults-requiring-tpn-and-experiencing-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "After successful initial treatment, all people with diabetes in hospital requiring total parenteral nutrition (TPN) and experiencing hypoglycaemia should be referred to a dietitian/nutrition team and/or inpatient diabetes team." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/relaxed-glycaemic-control-for-frail-elderly-people-with-multiple-comorbidities>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "It is important to emphasise the glycaemic target should be adjusted according to the person’s individual circumstances; a frail, elderly person with multiple comorbidities may benefit from less stringent glycaemic control." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/removal-of-infusion-set-after-correction-of-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Remove infusion set and discard once hypoglycaemia corrected" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/repeat-blood-glucose-measurement-after-carbohydrate-treatment>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "4. Repeat capillary blood glucose measurement 10-15 minutes later. If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L, repeat step 3 (no more than 3 treatments in total).  If it is above 4.0mmol/L then go to step 7." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/repeat-blood-glucose-measurement-after-glucagon-or-iv-glucose>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "6. Repeat capillary blood glucose measurement 10 minutes later. If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L, repeat step 5." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/repeat-capillary-blood-glucose-after-iv-glucose>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Repeat capillary blood glucose measurement 10 minutes later. If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L, repeat steps 3 and 4 if required." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/repeat-capillary-blood-glucose-after-treatment>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Repeat capillary blood glucose levels after 10-15 minutes. If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L repeat steps 1 and 3 (no more than 3 treatments in total and only give IM glucagon once)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/repeat-capillary-blood-glucose-measurement-10-minutes-after-treatment>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "7. Repeat capillary blood glucose measurement 10 minutes later. If it is still less than 4.0mmol/L, repeat step 5 (or 6 if IV access remains unavailable)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/repeated-administration-of-glucagon-is-not-advised>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Repeated administration of glucagon is not advised (and unlicensed), only 1% of people responded to a second injection of glucagon who had not responded to the first injection (61)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/replenishment-of-hypo-box-after-use> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Appendix 1 “Hypo box” contents should be checked on a daily basis to ensure it is complete and in date. It is the responsibility of the member of staff who uses any contents to replenish them after use." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/request-immediate-assistance-from-medical-staff-for-severe-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "3. Request immediate assistance from medical staff" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/restart-insulin-infusion-once-hypoglycaemia-is-fully-treated>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Restart the insulin infusion once the hypo has been fully treated." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/restock-hypo-boxes-if-used> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    """When hypoglycaemia has been successfully treated  
(after following the initial steps outlined in algorithms A-E) 

l	 Consider completing an incident form (e.g. DATIX) if appropriate. 
l	 If “hypo boxes” have been used then restock as appropriate.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/review-diabetes-treatment-after-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "This should also trigger a review of diabetes treatment to see if any adjustment is necessary." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/review-insulin-regimen-after-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """7. DO NOT omit insulin injection if due. The insulin injection about to be given is 
unlikely to be the insulin dose that was active at the time of the hypoglycaemia episode 
and so should not be omitted.  However, consideration will have to be given as to which 
insulin dose was active at the time of the hypoglycaemic episode and so a review of their 
insulin regimen is likely to be required.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/review-of-insulin-regimen-after-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "However, consideration will have to be given as to which insulin dose was active at the time of the hypoglycaemic episode and so a review of their insulin regimen is likely to be required." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/stop-infusion-if-hypersensitivity-reaction-occurs>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Stop the infusion immediately if any signs or symptoms of a suspected hypersensitivity reaction develop. Treat reaction and seek urgent medical advice regarding hypoglycaemia treatment" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/stop-insulin-infusion-during-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "2. If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately. Continue to follow the guidance below. Restart the insulin infusion once the hypo has been fully treated. Consider reviewing insulin infusion requirement and dosing" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/stop-insulin-infusion-immediately-if-in-situ-during-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/stop-insulin-infusion-in-hospitalized-patients-with-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "2. If the person with diabetes in hospital has an insulin infusion in situ, stop immediately. Continue to follow the guidance below. Restart the insulin infusion once the hypo has been fully treated. Consider reviewing insulin infusion requirement and dosing" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/storage-of-glucagon-pack> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "1x Glucagon pack – to be kept in the nearest drug fridge or labelled with reduced expiry date of 18 months if stored at room temperature" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/take-measures-to-avoid-future-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> """When hypoglycaemia has been successfully treated  
(after following the initial steps outlined in algorithms A-E) 

l	 Consider completing an incident form (e.g. DATIX) if appropriate. 
l	 If “hypo boxes” have been used then restock as appropriate. 
l	 Identify the risk factors for hypoglycaemia (see Table 2). 
l	 Take measures to avoid hypoglycaemia in the future.  The Inpatient Diabetes 
Team can be contacted to discuss this.""" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/treat-with-intravenous-glucose-algorithm-c-section-5>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Treat with intravenous glucose (algorithm C, section 5)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/treatment-of-hypoglycaemia-in-adults-with-diabetes-in-hospital>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "All non-pregnant, adults with diabetes in hospital with a blood glucose level less than 4.0mmol/L (with or without symptoms of hypoglycaemia) should be treated as outlined in this guideline; remember “4.0 is the floor”." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/treatment-of-hypoglycaemia-in-hospitalised-adults>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "1. Quickly check the following.  Don’t spend too much time on this, particularly if the person is otherwise well, before moving on to step 2:     a. Airway b. Breathing c. Circulation d. Disability (including Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) and blood glucose) e. Exposure (including temperature)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/treatment-without-delay-in-seizure-cases>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If measurement is difficult (e.g. in a person having a seizure) then treatment should not be delayed." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-10-glucose-infusion-for-hypoglycaemia-management>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "10% glucose - peripherally via a secure cannula into a large vein or central access (preferred where available). If peripheral infusion continues for more than 24 hours change infusion site to minimise thrombophlebitis. Care should be taken to ensure that the whole infusion is not inadvertently administered." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-10-glucose-infusion-for-persistent-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "For persistent hypoglycaemia despite appropriate initial treatment, an infusion of 10% glucose at 100ml/hr may be required." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-10-or-20-glucose-solutions-for-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "For these reasons 10% or 20% glucose solutions are preferred." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-20-glucose-infusion-for-emergency-management-of-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "20% glucose - short term peripheral use via a secure cannula into a large vein is acceptable for the emergency management of hypoglycaemia with close monitoring of the infusion site for thrombophlebitis.  Central access is preferred where available and is desirable if 20% infusion has to be continued after the initial dose." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-20g-of-rapid-acting-carbohydrate-for-treatment-of-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Vindedzis et al compared 15g versus 20g and found that 32-63% of episodes resolved after one treatment with 15g carbohydrate compared with 55-89% of episodes with 20g carbohydrate (46)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-40-glucose-gel-for-hypoglycaemia-in-non-cooperative-patients>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If the person is not capable and/or uncooperative, but is able to swallow, give 2 tubes 40% glucose gel (e.g. Glucogel®) squeezed into the mouth between the teeth and gums" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-computerised-approaches-to-reduce-inpatient-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "The hope is that the best performing algorithm could utilise available data in the electronic patient record to highlight those at risk of hypoglycaemia to the medical team (42)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-continuous-subcutaneous-insulin-infusions-for-inpatient-management>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If people in hospital are to continue using their CSII devices, they must be well enough to self-manage their diabetes. If they are not, then alternatives such as a variable rate insulin infusion or subcutaneous insulin administration should be considered." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-infusion-pump-for-glucose-administration>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "In all other situations, an infusion pump is required." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-infusion-pump-for-non-emergency-hypoglycaemia-management>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "In all other situations, an infusion pump is required." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-intravenous-glucose-for-fasting-patients>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "If the person is fasting for a procedure or operation, then consider using intravenous glucose to avoid the operation or procedure being postponed due to oral carbohydrate consumption." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-orange-juice-with-caution-in-people-with-ckd-stage-4-or-5>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Orange juice (which contains fructose) remains a popular treatment for hypoglycaemia although caution should be exercised in people with chronic kidney disease (CKD) stage 4 or CKD stage 5 due to the potassium content." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/use-of-rapid-acting-carbohydrate-for-conscious-patients>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "In people who are conscious and able to swallow, 15-20g of rapid-acting carbohydrate is the treatment of choice (see algorithm A)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/recommendation/wait-at-least-10-minutes-before-re-testing-after-hypoglycaemia-treatment>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "All are supportive of a minimum interval of at least 10 minutes before re-testing to ensure resolution of hypoglycaemia (53)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/acute-discontinuation-of-long-term-corticosteroid-therapy>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Acute discontinuation of long-term corticosteroid therapy" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/addison-s-disease> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Endocrine disorders associated with diminished counterregulatory responses (e.g. Addison’s disease, growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/age-70-years> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Multivariable logistic regression analysis showed that age >70 years, cognitive dysfunction and nephropathy were independently associated with hypoglycaemia for people in hospital with T2DM (35)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/age-75-years> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "A British study used a similar technique to show that age >75 years, insulin & sulfonylurea therapy, black and Asian ethnicity, emergency (rather than elective) admission, lower eGFR, raised inflammatory markers, hyponatraemia (<125mmol/L) and low albumin were the strongest predictors of hypoglycaemia in people with diabetes admitted to medical or surgical wards (not intensive therapy units) (36)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/bariatric-surgery-involving-bowel-resection>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Bariatric surgery involving bowel resection" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/black-and-asian-ethnicity> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "A British study used a similar technique to show that age >75 years, insulin & sulfonylurea therapy, black and Asian ethnicity, emergency (rather than elective) admission, lower eGFR, raised inflammatory markers, hyponatraemia (<125mmol/L) and low albumin were the strongest predictors of hypoglycaemia in people with diabetes admitted to medical or surgical wards (not intensive therapy units) (36)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/blocked-displaced-feeding-tube> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Blocked/displaced feeding tube" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/change-of-insulin-injection-site> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Change of insulin injection site" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/changes-in-medication-that-cause-hyperglycaemia-e-g-steroid-therapy-reduced-stopped>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Changes in medication that cause hyperglycaemia e.g. steroid therapy reduced/stopped" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/cognitive-dysfunction> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Multivariable logistic regression analysis showed that age >70 years, cognitive dysfunction and nephropathy were independently associated with hypoglycaemia for people in hospital with T2DM (35)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/concurrent-use-of-medicines-with-hypoglycaemic-agents>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Concurrent use of medicines with hypoglycaemic agents e.g. warfarin, quinine, salicylates, fibrates, sulphonamides (including cotrimoxazole), linezolid, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), probenecid, somatostatin analogues, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/confusing-the-insulin-name-with-the-dose> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Confusing the insulin name with the dose (e.g. Humalog Mix25 becoming Humalog 25 units)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/delirium-secondary-to-a-chest-infection> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "He has been admitted with delirium secondary to a chest infection. His temporary cognitive impairment is likely to make self-management more difficult." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/deterioration-in-renal-function> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Deterioration in renal function" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/diabetes-medication-administered-at-an-inappropriate-time-to-feed>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Diabetes medication administered at an inappropriate time to feed" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/duration-of-insulin-therapy-in-type-2-diabetes>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Duration of insulin therapy in type 2 diabetes" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/emergency-admission> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "A British study used a similar technique to show that age >75 years, insulin & sulfonylurea therapy, black and Asian ethnicity, emergency (rather than elective) admission, lower eGFR, raised inflammatory markers, hyponatraemia (<125mmol/L) and low albumin were the strongest predictors of hypoglycaemia in people with diabetes admitted to medical or surgical wards (not intensive therapy units) (36)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/enteral-feed-discontinued> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Enteral feed discontinued" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/failure-to-monitor-blood-glucose-adequately>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Failure to monitor blood glucose adequately" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/food-malabsorption> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Food malabsorption e.g. gastroenteritis, coeliac disease, gastroparesis" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/frailty-with-cognitive-impairment> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Particular care must be taken with older people (particularly those who are frail with cognitive impairment) as the clinical features may be subtle and mistaken for other problems." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/growth-hormone-deficiency> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Endocrine disorders associated with diminished counterregulatory responses (e.g. Addison’s disease, growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism)",
    "growth hormone deficiency" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/hypoglycaemia-3-0mmol-l> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "The high risk of recurrence offers an opportunity for intervention in people experiencing hypoglycaemia while in hospital." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/hyponatraemia-125mmol-l> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "A British study used a similar technique to show that age >75 years, insulin & sulfonylurea therapy, black and Asian ethnicity, emergency (rather than elective) admission, lower eGFR, raised inflammatory markers, hyponatraemia (<125mmol/L) and low albumin were the strongest predictors of hypoglycaemia in people with diabetes admitted to medical or surgical wards (not intensive therapy units) (36)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/hypopituitarism> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Endocrine disorders associated with diminished counterregulatory responses (e.g. Addison’s disease, growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/impaired-awareness-of-hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia (IAH) is an acquired syndrome associated with insulin treatment. IAH results in the warning symptoms of hypoglycaemia becoming diminished in intensity, altered in nature or lost altogether. This increases the vulnerability of affected individuals of progression to severe hypoglycaemia." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/impaired-renal-function> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Impaired renal function (including those people requiring renal replacement therapy)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/inadequate-mixing-of-intermediate-acting-or-mixed-insulins>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Inadequate mixing of intermediate-acting or mixed insulins",
    "nadequate mixing of intermediate-acting or mixed insulinsmixed insulins" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/inadequate-treatment-of-previous-hypoglycaemia>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Inadequate treatment of previous hypoglycaemia" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/incorrect-drug-history-and-failure-to-correctly-reconcile-on-admission>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Incorrect drug history and failure to correctly reconcile on admission" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/incorrect-type-of-insulin-or-oral-hypoglycaemia-therapy-prescribed-and-administered>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "Incorrect type of insulin or oral hypoglycaemia therapy prescribed and administered" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/increased-exercise-relative-to-usual> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Increased exercise (relative to usual)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/insulin-sulfonylurea-therapy> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "A British study used a similar technique to show that age >75 years, insulin & sulfonylurea therapy, black and Asian ethnicity, emergency (rather than elective) admission, lower eGFR, raised inflammatory markers, hyponatraemia (<125mmol/L) and low albumin were the strongest predictors of hypoglycaemia in people with diabetes admitted to medical or surgical wards (not intensive therapy units) (36)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/insulin-therapy> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Promote the prescription of hypoglycaemia “rescue” treatment for all people with diabetes in hospital on insulin or sulfonylurea therapy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/insulin-treated-type-2-diabetes-mellitus> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "While severe hypoglycaemia is less common in people with insulin-treated type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM), people with insulin-treated T2DM are more likely to require hospital admission as part of their treatment than those with T1DM (12,13)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/iv-insulin-infusion-with-or-without-glucose-infusion>
  <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote> "IV insulin infusion with or without glucose infusion" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/less-carbohydrate-than-normal> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "• Less carbohydrate than normal" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/lipohypertrophy-at-injection-sites> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Lipohypertrophy at injection sites" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/long-acting-insulin-therapy> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "If the hypoglycaemia was caused by sulfonylurea or long-acting insulin therapy then be aware the risk of hypoglycaemia may persist for up to 24-36 hours following the last dose, especially if there is concurrent renal impairment." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/long-duration-of-type-1-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "Long duration of type 1 diabetes" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/low-albumin> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
    "A British study used a similar technique to show that age >75 years, insulin & sulfonylurea therapy, black and Asian ethnicity, emergency (rather than elective) admission, lower eGFR, raised inflammatory markers, hyponatraemia (<125mmol/L) and low albumin were the strongest predictors of hypoglycaemia in people with diabetes admitted to medical or surgical wards (not intensive therapy units) (36)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/lower-egfr> <https://example.org/dmo#evidenceQuote>
