<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-1-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "RAS inhibition with ACEi or ARBs has been standard of care in patients with T1D and T2D and CKD for decades.",
    "DKA is most common among people with type 1 diabetes.", "Low blood sugar is especially common in people with type 1 diabetes.",
    "People with type 1 diabetes must use insulin.", "Hypoglycaemia is defined as blood glucose <4 mmol/L. Prevention and management of hypoglycaemia must be considered when planning and during the perioperative and postoperative phases of surgery in all those at risk (on insulin and insulin secretagogues)",
    "If you have type 1 diabetes, your body makes little or no insulin. Your immune system attacks and destroys the cells in your pancreas that make insulin.",
    "DKA is most common in people with type 1 diabetes.", "If you have type 1 diabetes, you must take insulin because your pancreas does not make it.",
    "If you have type 1 diabetes, your health care team may ask you to check your urine at home for substances called ketones.",
    "Your health care professional will order a test for autoantibodies to find out if you have type 1 diabetes.",
    "NIDDK-supported research helped scientists find genes linked to type 1 diabetes and new ways to identify people with type 1 diabetes before they develop symptoms.",
    "If you have diabetes, your doctor may look for certain autoantibodies to see if your diabetes is type 1. Autoantibodies are proteins in your immune system that mistakenly attack your healthy tissues and cells.",
    "patients with type 1 diabetes for five years or more", "Impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia (IAH) is an acquired syndrome associated with insulin treatment. IAH results in the warning symptoms of hypoglycaemia becoming diminished in intensity, altered in nature or lost altogether. This increases the vulnerability of affected individuals of progression to severe hypoglycaemia. The prevalence of IAH increases with duration of diabetes and is more common in type 1 than in type 2 diabetes (31).",
    "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is a frequent and potentially life-threatening complication of type 1 diabetes.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "type-1-diabetes" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-2-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "RAS inhibition with ACEi or ARBs has been standard of care in patients with T1D and T2D and CKD for decades.",
    "People with type 2 diabetes can also develop DKA.", "Prediabetes is a serious health condition that increases your risk of developing type 2 diabetes, heart disease, and stroke.",
    "These medicines are most often used to treat type 2 diabetes.", "Screen for type 2 diabetes in women who had GDM",
    "The role of metabolic-bariatric surgery as effective interventions for obesity and type 2 diabetes is well recognised, particularly in achieving type 2 diabetes remission or improvement in glycaemic control, treatment impact and improvements in associated complications",
    "The A1C test can be used to diagnose type 2 diabetes and prediabetes.", "If you have type 2 diabetes, the cells in your body don’t use insulin properly. The pancreas may be making insulin but is not making enough insulin to keep your blood glucose level in the normal range.",
    "Diagnosis of type 2 diabetes is definitively made by blood tests. Diagnosis requires two abnormal test results from the same sample or from two different samples.",
    "You should get tested every year for kidney disease if you have type 2 diabetes",
    "If you have type 2 diabetes, your health care professional may recommend diabetes medicines that may help you lose weight.",
    "Some people with type 2 diabetes can control their blood glucose level by making lifestyle changes.",
    "However, some people with type 2 diabetes can develop ketoacidosis if they do not produce enough insulin.",
    "Health care professionals will diagnose diabetes using your symptoms, medical and family history, and diabetes tests. Your health care professional may also order tests to find out if your diabetes is type 1, type 2, or monogenic.",
    "Some people develop diabetes during pregnancy that goes away after they have the baby. This type of diabetes is called gestational diabetes. However, having gestational diabetes increases your risk of getting type 2 diabetes later in life.",
    "Type 2 diabetes develops when your pancreas doesn’t produce enough insulin, and your body has trouble using insulin, a condition called insulin resistance.",
    "The oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) helps doctors detect type 2 diabetes, prediabetes, and gestational diabetes.",
    "patients with type 2 diabetes", "Multivariable logistic regression analysis showed that age >70 years, cognitive dysfunction and nephropathy were independently associated with hypoglycaemia for people in hospital with T2DM (35).",
    "DKA does not exclusively occur in people with type 1 diabetes, and people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA – so called ‘ketosis prone type 2 diabetes’ (11).";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "type-2-diabetes" .

