<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/a1c-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The A1C test is a blood test that provides information about your average levels of blood glucose, also called blood sugar, over the past 3 months.",
    "The A1C test shows your average blood glucose level over the past 3 months.", "Screening and diagnosis of prediabetes; Screening and diagnosis of type 2 diabetes; Monitoring of diabetes",
    "A blood test that measures your average blood glucose level over the past 2 to 3 months.",
    "The A1C test is a blood test that provides your average levels of blood glucose over the last 3 months. Other names for the A1C test are hemoglobin A1C, HbA1C, glycated hemoglobin, and glycosylated hemoglobin test. You can eat and drink before this test. Before using the A1C test to diagnose diabetes, your doctor will consider factors, such as whether you are in your second or third trimester of pregnancy or whether you have certain types of anemia NIH external link or another problem with your blood.1 The A1C test might not be accurate in those cases." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/estimated-average-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Estimated average glucose (eAG) is calculated from your A1C and helps you relate your A1C to daily glucose monitoring levels. It converts the A1C percentage to the same units used by home glucose meters—milligrams per deciliter (mg/dL)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/fasting-plasma-glucose-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "For these blood glucose tests used to diagnose diabetes, you must fast at least 8 hours before you have your blood drawn.",
    "The fasting plasma glucose (FPG) test measures your blood glucose level at a single point in time. For the most reliable results, your doctor will give you the test in the morning after you have fasted for at least 8 hours. Fasting means having nothing to eat or drink except sips of water." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/fpg> <https://example.org/dmo#definition> "Fasting plasma glucose (FPG) test measures blood glucose after at least 8 hours of fasting." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/ogtt-2h> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) at 2 hours measures blood glucose levels 2 hours after consuming a glucose solution following an overnight fast." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/oral-glucose-tolerance-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The glucose challenge test or the oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) are used to check for gestational diabetes, usually between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy.",
    "A test used to confirm the diagnosis of gestational diabetes after a positive glucose challenge test. It measures blood glucose levels before and after consuming a glucose solution.",
    "The oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT) helps doctors detect type 2 diabetes, prediabetes, and gestational diabetes." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/random-plasma-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Random plasma glucose test measures blood glucose at any time of day, regardless of when the person last ate, and is used when symptoms of diabetes are present." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/random-plasma-glucose-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "If you have symptoms of diabetes, your doctor may use the random plasma glucose test, which doesn’t require fasting.",
    "Sometimes doctors use the random plasma glucose test to diagnose diabetes when you have symptoms of diabetes and they do not want to wait until you have fasted for 8 hours." .

