<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/cataract> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A condition in which the lens of the eye becomes cloudy, leading to vision impairment." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/cataracts> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The lenses within our eyes are clear structures that help provide sharp vision—but they tend to become cloudy as we age. People with diabetes are more likely to develop cloudy lenses, called cataracts." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/detached-retina> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "These changes in your sight can be symptoms of a detached retina NIH external link, which is a medical emergency." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/diabetic-macular-edema> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The part of your retina that you need for reading, driving, and seeing faces is called the macula. Diabetes can lead to swelling in the macula, which is called diabetic macular edema." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/glaucoma> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Glaucoma is a group of eye diseases that can damage the optic nerve—the bundle of nerves that connects the eye to the brain." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/nonproliferative-diabetic-retinopathy> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "In early diabetic retinopathy, blood vessels can weaken, bulge, or leak into the retina. This stage is called nonproliferative diabetic retinopathy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/proliferative-diabetic-retinopathy> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "If the disease gets worse, some blood vessels close off, which causes new blood vessels to grow, or proliferate, on the surface of the retina. This stage is called proliferative diabetic retinopathy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/retinal-detachment> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A condition in which the retina peels away from the tissue beneath it, which can cause blindness." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-1> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Type 1 diabetes is a condition in which the body does not produce insulin, and people with this type of diabetes must take insulin to survive.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "type-1" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-2> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Type 2 diabetes is a condition in which the body does not use insulin properly, and people with this type of diabetes may or may not require insulin.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "type-2" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/charcot-s-foot> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Although rare, nerve damage from diabetes can lead to changes in the shape of your feet, such as Charcot’s foot. Charcot’s foot may start with redness, warmth, and swelling. Later, bones in your feet and toes can shift or break, which can cause your feet to have an odd shape, such as a “rocker bottom.”" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/foot-ulcer> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "a cut, blister, or bruise on your foot that does not start to heal after a few days" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/gangrene> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The infection might lead to gangrene." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/peripheral-artery-disease> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "peripheral artery disease" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/peripheral-neuropathy> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "you may burn your feet and not know you did", "Peripheral neuropathy is nerve damage that typically affects the feet and legs and sometimes affects the hands and arms." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/autonomic-neuropathy> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Autonomic neuropathy is damage to nerves that control your internal organs. Autonomic neuropathy can lead to problems with your heart rate and blood pressure, digestive system, bladder, sex organs, sweat glands, eyes, and ability to sense hypoglycemia." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/balance-and-coordination-problems> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Nerve damage can also cause problems with balance and coordination, leading to falls and fractures." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/focal-neuropathies> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Focal neuropathies are conditions in which you typically have damage to single nerves, most often in your hand, head, torso, and leg." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/foot-complications> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Peripheral neuropathy can lead to foot complications, such as sores, ulcers, and infections, because nerve damage can make you lose feeling in your feet." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/proximal-neuropathy> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Proximal neuropathy is a rare and disabling type of nerve damage in your hip, buttock, or thigh. This type of nerve damage typically affects one side of your body and may rarely spread to the other side. Proximal neuropathy often causes severe pain and may lead to significant weight loss." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/glucose-challenge-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A test used to screen for gestational diabetes, performed between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy. If the results show high blood glucose, an oral glucose tolerance test is required to confirm the diagnosis.",
    "In this test, a health care professional will take a sample of your blood 1 hour after you drink a sweet liquid containing glucose." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/foot-problems> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "People with diabetes may have problems with their feet because high blood glucose levels can damage blood vessels and nerves." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/low-blood-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Low blood glucose levels may last for hours or days after physical activity. You are most likely to have low blood glucose if you take insulin or some other diabetes medicines, such as sulfonylureas.",
    "Very low blood glucose may lead to confusion or fainting NIH external link, and it can become a serious medical emergency that must be treated right away.",
    "Hypoglycemia occurs when your blood glucose drops too low." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/blindness> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Blindness is a health problem from diabetes that can be prevented or delayed by managing your ABCs." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/foot-or-leg-amputation> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Foot or leg amputation is a health problem from diabetes that can be prevented or delayed by managing your ABCs." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/nerve-disease> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "participants with type 1 diabetes who kept their blood glucose levels close to normal greatly lowered their chances of developing eye, kidney, and nerve disease" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-2-diabetes-in-adolescents-and-youth>
  <https://example.org/dmo#definition> "The TODAY studies, both funded by NIDDK, show the importance of early, intensive treatment and blood glucose management for children or adolescents diagnosed with type 2 diabetes.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "type-2-diabetes-in-adolescents-and-youth" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/a1c-blood-glucose-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The A1C test shows your average blood glucose level over the last 3 months." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/cholesterol-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Cholesterol is a fat, also called lipid, that is produced by your liver. Unhealthy levels of cholesterol in your blood can build up and clog your blood vessels, which may result in a heart attack or a stroke." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/cancer> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetes is also linked to some types of cancer.", "Diabetes is linked to some types of cancer External link." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/eye-damage> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetes may raise your risk of eye, kidney, nerve, and heart damage." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/foot-damage> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetes may raise your risk of eye, kidney, nerve, and heart damage." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/heart-damage> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetes may raise your risk of eye, kidney, nerve, and heart damage." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/kidney-damage> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetes may raise your risk of eye, kidney, nerve, and heart damage." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/nerve-damage> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetes may raise your risk of eye, kidney, nerve, and heart damage." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/tooth-damage> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetes may raise your risk of eye, kidney, nerve, and heart damage." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/maturity-onset-diabetes-of-the-young-mody>
  <https://example.org/dmo#definition> "MODY forms of diabetes usually develop in teens or young adults.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "maturity-onset-diabetes-of-the-young-mody" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/mody> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Most people with MODY forms of diabetes have inherited a gene for the disease from a parent who also had the disease.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "mody" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/ndm> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Some forms of NDM or MODY occur only if a child inherits a gene for the disease from both parents.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "ndm" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/neonatal-diabetes-mellitus-ndm> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "NDM forms of diabetes develop in the first 6 to 12 months of life.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "neonatal-diabetes-mellitus-ndm" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/autoantibodies> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Proteins made by your immune system that attack your healthy tissues and cells by mistake." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/genetic-testing> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Looks for gene variants." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/birth-defects> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "High blood glucose levels can harm the baby during this early stage and increase the chance that your baby will have birth defects of any of these organs." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/diabetic-eye-disease> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetic eye disease can get worse during your pregnancy, and you may need special care.",
    "Diabetes can damage your eyes and lead to low vision and blindness." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/high-blood-glucose-levels> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "High blood glucose levels can harm your baby during the first weeks of pregnancy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/low-blood-glucose-levels> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Blood glucose levels can drop very quickly after delivery." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/miscarriage> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "High blood glucose levels can also increase the chance that you will have a miscarriage" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/obesity> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "If you have diabetes and your blood glucose levels are high, your baby also has an increased risk of developing some health problems later in life, such as obesity" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/preeclampsia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "In preeclampsia, your blood pressure goes up and you may have too much protein in your urine during the second half of pregnancy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/preterm-birth> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "High blood glucose levels during pregnancy can also increase the chance that your baby will be born too early" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/a1c-blood-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Results of the A1C test reflect your average blood glucose levels during the last 3 months." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose-levels> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Blood glucose levels can drop very quickly after delivery. You should ask your health care team what changes should be made to your medicines. You should also check your blood glucose levels often to watch for low blood glucose levels in the first few days." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/dementia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "High blood glucose increases the chance of developing dementia NIH external link." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/depression> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Depression NIH external link is common among people with a chronic, or long-term, illness such as diabetes." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/diabetes-foot-problems> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetes can cause nerve damage and poor blood flow, which can lead to serious foot problems." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/diabetes-gum-disease-other-dental-problems>
  <https://example.org/dmo#definition> "Diabetes can lead to problems in your mouth, such as infection, gum disease, or dry mouth." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/diabetes-sexual-bladder-problems> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Sexual and bladder problems are more common in people with diabetes. Problems like erectile dysfunction, loss of interest in sex, bladder leaks, and retained urine can happen if diabetes damages your blood vessels and nerves." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/sleep-apnea> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "People who have sleep apnea NIH external link—when you stop breathing for short periods during sleep—are more likely to develop type 2 diabetes." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/acromegaly> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "acromegaly, which can cause the body to produce too much growth hormone";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "acromegaly" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/antiseizure-medicines> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "antiseizure medicines that are used to treat epilepsy";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "antiseizure-medicines" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/cushing-s-syndrome> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Cushing’s syndrome, which can cause the body to produce too much cortisol—often called the “stress hormone”";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "cushing-s-syndrome" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/diabetes-related-to-acute-pancreatitis-and-its-mechanisms>
  <https://example.org/dmo#definition> "As part of this group, the Diabetes Related to Acute Pancreatitis and Its Mechanisms (DREAM) study is looking at people with recent acute pancreatitis to see how many develop diabetes.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "diabetes-related-to-acute-pancreatitis-and-its-mechanisms" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/hyperthyroidism> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "hyperthyroidism, which can cause the body to produce too much thyroid hormone";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "hyperthyroidism" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/hypothyroidism> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "hypothyroidism, which can cause the body to produce too little thyroid hormone";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "hypothyroidism" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/immunosuppressants> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "immunosuppressants that are used after an organ transplant";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "immunosuppressants" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/pancreatitis> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The pancreas can be damaged by injury or diseases such as inflammation of the pancreas, also called pancreatitis.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "pancreatitis" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/fasting-plasma-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Before the test, you will need to fast for at least 8 hours. A health care professional will take a blood sample to measure your glucose level after fasting." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/oral-glucose-tolerance-test-2h> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Another blood sample is taken 2 hours later to check your blood glucose level." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/cognitive-dysfunction-dementia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Cognitive dysfunction/dementia" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hyperosmolar-hyperglycaemic-state> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The management of the hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS) in adults with diabetes",
    "HHS: Biochemical markers have normalised and STILL not eating and drinking" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/impaired-awareness-of-hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia (IAH) is an acquired syndrome associated with insulin treatment. IAH results in the warning symptoms of hypoglycaemia becoming diminished in intensity, altered in nature or lost altogether." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/looming-hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The risk of looming hypoglycaemia should be considered in people who have a blood glucose level of 4.0-6.0mmol/L while in hospital." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/nocturnal-hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Nocturnal hypoglycaemia (blood glucose <3.9mmol/L) was more common in people treated with sulfonylurea therapy than insulin therapy (75.3% versus 59.3% respectively)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/severe-hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "This increases the vulnerability of affected individuals of progression to severe hypoglycaemia." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/t1dm> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "T1DM is a type of diabetes that is typically diagnosed in childhood and is characterized by absolute insulin deficiency due to autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "t1dm" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/t2dm> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "T2DM is a type of diabetes that is typically diagnosed in adulthood and is characterized by insulin resistance and relative insulin deficiency.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "t2dm" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/t2dm-diet-only> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A middle-aged man with T2DM on metformin with an HbA1c usually around 48mmol/L would not be expected to be at high risk of hypoglycaemia";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "t2dm-diet-only" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/t2dm-insulin> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "An 84-year-old woman with T2DM on high dose sulfonylurea therapy admitted with a urinary tract infection and acute kidney injury would be at substantially higher risk of hypoglycaemia.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "t2dm-insulin" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/t2dm-tablets> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A middle-aged man with T2DM on metformin with an HbA1c usually around 48mmol/L would not be expected to be at high risk of hypoglycaemia";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "t2dm-tablets" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose-level> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A measure of the concentration of glucose in the blood, used to diagnose and monitor hypoglycaemia and hyperglycaemia." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A measure of blood glucose concentration obtained from capillary blood, used to assess hypoglycaemia and guide treatment.",
    "The concentration of glucose in capillary blood, which should be reduced by 3.0 mmol/L/hour during treatment of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-glucose-measurement> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A measurement of blood glucose level taken from capillary blood, used to assess hypoglycaemia in acutely ill adults as part of the ABCDE approach." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/hypoglycaemia-2-9mmol-l> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A severe hypoglycemic episode defined by blood glucose levels below 2.9mmol/L, most likely to occur overnight and in the morning in hospital settings." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/hypoglycaemia-3-0mmol-l> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A hypoglycemic episode defined by blood glucose levels below 3.0mmol/L, associated with increased risk of recurrence and clinical intervention." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/looming-hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A clinical state where blood glucose levels are between 4.0 and 6.0mmol/L, indicating a potential risk of impending hypoglycemia, especially in patients on insulin secretagogues or insulin therapy." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/nocturnal-hypoglycaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Hypoglycemia occurring during sleep, defined as blood glucose <3.9mmol/L." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/acute-pancreatitis> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Other, rare complications include pulmonary oedema; a rise in pancreatic enzymes, with or without acute pancreatitis; cardiomyopathy; rhabdomyolysis; and, gastrointestinal bleeding (25)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/acute-pre-renal-kidney-injury> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Because of the risk of acute pre-renal kidney injury associated with severe dehydration, it is recommended that no potassium be prescribed with the initial fluid resuscitation or if the serum potassium level remains above 5.5 mmol/L." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/cardiomyopathy> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Other, rare complications include pulmonary oedema; a rise in pancreatic enzymes, with or without acute pancreatitis; cardiomyopathy; rhabdomyolysis; and, gastrointestinal bleeding (25)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/cerebral-oedema> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Cerebral oedema causing symptoms is relatively uncommon in adults, although may occur in those who are physically slight or in younger adults." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/diabetic-keto-acidosis> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "For use for ALL ADULT patients with a diagnosis of DKA NOT FOR USE IN CHILDREN" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/euglycaemic-dka> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "This is the development of DKA in people known to have diabetes but where the glucose is normal, or not particularly raised." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/fluid-overload> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "a condition resulting from excessive intravenous fluid administration, which can occur during DKA treatment" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/gastrointestinal-bleeding> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Other, rare complications include pulmonary oedema; a rise in pancreatic enzymes, with or without acute pancreatitis; cardiomyopathy; rhabdomyolysis; and, gastrointestinal bleeding (25)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hyperkalaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Hypokalaemia and hyperkalaemia are potentially life-threatening conditions during the management of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/hypokalaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Hypokalaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) was reported in 67% of cases in a national survey of DKA management." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/ketosis-prone-type-2-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "DKA does not exclusively occur in people with type 1 diabetes, and people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA – so called ‘ketosis prone type 2 diabetes’ (11)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/pulmonary-oedema> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Other, rare complications include pulmonary oedema; a rise in pancreatic enzymes, with or without acute pancreatitis; cardiomyopathy; rhabdomyolysis; and, gastrointestinal bleeding (25)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/rhabdomyolysis> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Other, rare complications include pulmonary oedema; a rise in pancreatic enzymes, with or without acute pancreatitis; cardiomyopathy; rhabdomyolysis; and, gastrointestinal bleeding (25)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/transient-acute-kidney-injury> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Transient acute kidney injury may occur in up to 50% of adults (25)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/venous-thromboembolic-disease> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Several other complications may occur with some being relatively common, generally mild and easily treated. However, others may be more serious. These include the development of venous thromboembolic disease, particularly if central venous catheters are used." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/diabetic-ketoacidosis-in-pregnancy> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Diabetic ketoacidosis is a medical emergency requiring prompt treatment, and is different to a ketosis of pregnancy. DKA in pregnancy is an obstetric emergency as it carries a risk of mortality and morbidity for the pregnant woman and the fetus (59).";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "diabetic-ketoacidosis-in-pregnancy" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/euglycaemic-dka> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "DKA in pregnancy can occur with only very modest elevation of glucose levels, i.e. <13.9mmol/l, (‘euglycaemic DKA’) in women during pregnancy.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "euglycaemic-dka" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/ketosis-prone-type-2-diabetes> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "This guidance also addresses the new problem of DKA and particularly euglycaemic DKA in those treated with SGLT-2 inhibitors. It also for the first time considers ketosis prone type 2 diabetes and the complex issue of the management of DKA in people with end stage renal failure or on dialysis.";
  <https://example.org/dmo#diabetesTypeCode> "ketosis-prone-type-2-diabetes" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/3-beta-hydroxybutyrate> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "the main blood ketone" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/anion-gap> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A measure used to assess the balance of electrolytes in the blood, which was not significantly different in people with end stage renal disease compared to those with preserved renal function." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/arterial-blood-gases> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Arterial blood gases are measured if oxygen saturation is less than 92%." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/bicarbonate> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "should be assessed at or near the bedside using point of care testing" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/bicarbonate-concentration> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A bicarbonate concentration of <15.0 mmol/L is required for the diagnosis of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose-concentration> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A blood glucose concentration of >11.0 mmol/L is required for the diagnosis of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose-concentrations> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Measurement of blood glucose concentrations 24 hours per day – ideally using point of care testing meters" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketone> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Measurement of ketone levels in blood to assess the response to treatment in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketone-concentration> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "The concentration of ketones in the blood, which should be reduced by 0.5 mmol/L/hour during treatment of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketone-concentrations> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Measurement of blood ketone concentrations 24 hours per day – ideally using point of care testing meters" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketone-meters> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Used for point of care testing of blood ketones" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-ketonaemia> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Capillary blood ketonaemia on Trust approved ketone meter of ≥ 3 mmol/L" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-ketones> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Capillary blood ketones are measured hourly until DKA is resolved." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Measure blood ketones and capillary glucose hourly (note: if meter reads “blood glucose over 20 mmol/L” or “Hi” venous blood should be sent to the laboratory hourly or measured using venous blood in a blood gas analyser until the point of care testing meter is within its QA range)" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-ketone-concentration> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A capillary or blood ketone concentration of >3.0 mmol/L is required for the diagnosis of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-ketones> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Capillary ketones are measured hourly to assess response to treatment in DKA. A fall of at least 0.5 mmol/L/hr is expected." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/covid-19-testing> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Testing for SARS-CoV-2 infection, particularly in individuals without prior diabetes diagnosis, as infection may trigger DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/creatinine> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Creatinine is checked at 2 hours then 2 to 4 hourly until venous bicarbonate >15 mmol/L." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/electrolyte> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Electrolyte is checked at 2 hours then 2 to 4 hourly until venous bicarbonate >15 mmol/L." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/glasgow-coma-scale-score> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A neurological scale used to assess the level of consciousness, where any deterioration should prompt urgent treatment and imaging." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Monitored as part of the biochemical and metabolic parameters during DKA management." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/ketonuria> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Significant ketonuria (2+ or more on standard urine sticks) is required for the diagnosis of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/osmolality> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Osmolality is checked for HHS normalization as part of exit criteria." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/pancreatic-enzymes> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Enzymes produced by the pancreas, which may rise with or without acute pancreatitis in the context of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/ph> <https://example.org/dmo#definition> "A measure of the acidity or alkalinity of the blood, which was not significantly different in people with end stage renal disease compared to those with preserved renal function." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/plasma-glucose> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Plasma glucose is monitored hourly. A fall of at least 3 mmol/L/hr is expected. If glucose falls below 14 mmol/L, 10% glucose is added." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/potassium> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Potassium is monitored hourly. If outside normal range, re-assess replacement and check hourly. Abnormal levels require immediate senior advice." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/pregnancy-test> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Test to detect pregnancy in women of childbearing age, as pregnancy can precipitate or complicate DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/serum-potassium> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Serum potassium concentration may be normal or even elevated due to the extracellular shift of potassium in acidotic conditions, and this very poorly reflects total potassium stores." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/urinalysis-and-culture> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Urinalysis to detect urinary tract infection or other abnormalities, and culture to identify pathogens." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/urine-output> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Minimum urine output should be 0.5 ml/kg/hr to assess renal function and fluid balance." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/venous-bicarbonate> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Venous bicarbonate is measured at 60 minutes, 2 hours and 2 hourly thereafter to assess acidosis resolution. A rise of at least 3 mmol/L/hr is expected." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/venous-blood> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "if meter reads “blood glucose over 20 mmol/L” or “Hi” venous blood should be sent to the laboratory hourly or measured using venous blood in a blood gas analyser until the point of care testing meter is within its QA range" .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/venous-blood-gas> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Analysis of venous blood for pH, bicarbonate, and potassium to assess acid-base status and electrolyte balance in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/venous-k> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "Venous potassium (K+) is monitored as part of U&Es at 6, 12, and 24 hours to assess electrolyte balance during DKA treatment." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/venous-ph> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A venous pH <7.3 is required for the diagnosis of DKA." .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/ß-hydroxybutyrate-concentrations> <https://example.org/dmo#definition>
    "A measure of ketone levels in the blood, which were lower in those presenting in DKA with end stage renal disease compared to those with preserved renal function." .

